  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Below average temperatures and dry weather expected \ntoday. \n\nA chilly N-NW flow will continue overnight into this morning across \nthe region. As the cooler air flows across the relatively warm \nwaters of Lake America we will see some lake induced clouds \nsoutheast of Lake. \n\nHigh pressure centered over Michigan will drift to the Mid- \nAtlantic region today. Given the chilly airmass over the region \ntemperatures will once again peak in the upper 40s across the \nTug Hill region, to a range of 50s to near 60F elsewhere. \n\nTonight...light southerly flow will develop on the western flank of \nthe departed ridge which will begin the warming process as we work \ninto Wednesday. While we should see decent radiational cooling \novernight frost potential will be limited, if any at all for most \nlocations. Overall, lows will be found in the 30s to low 40s.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Warmer weather returns Wednesday along with a chance \nof showers by Wednesday night into Thursday. \n\nSoutherly flow ahead of an eastward advancing cold front will pump a \nwealth of much warmer air into the eastern Great Lakes Wednesday. So \nmuch so...we should see a rapid warm up with highs in the 60s \nto around 70F. The next chance of showers will come as a \nenergetic shortwave cuts across the upper Great Lakes. Guidance\nsuggest we may see some showers by Wednesday evening and on \nThursday as the cold front crosses the region. With the passage \nof the front a cooler airmass will once again spill into the \neastern Great Lakes Thursday night into Friday. The cooler \nweather looks like it will be short-lived as we could be looking\nat a significant warm up over the weekend.",
